An aircraft turbojet engine is a pure‑gas‑turbine propulsion unit that creates thrust by accelerating all intake air through combustion and expelling it via a convergent‑divergent nozzle at supersonic velocity. Unlike turbofan designs that blend bypass air for fuel efficiency, turbojets compress the entire airflow stream, delivering maximum specific thrust at high Mach numbers and altitudes while maintaining a compact, lightweight architecture-attributes prized by fifth‑generation fighters, unmanned combat drones and experimental hypersonic platforms where acceleration outweighs fuel economy.

